<main class="article">
<h1>KF-L001-B04 · Governing Knowledge Change</h1>
<p class="deck">How Knowledge Change Becomes Legitimate</p>
<p class="docmeta">Evidence &#183; Final &#183; v1.0</p>

<pre><code>Question  →  Corpus  →  Evidence  →  Patterns  →  Candidate Insight</code></pre>
<h3 id="purpose">Purpose</h3>
<p>To examine the principal intellectual traditions concerned with governing knowledge change, and to evaluate how different disciplines preserve trust, legitimacy, provenance, authenticity and continuity as knowledge evolves over time. This chapter writes out the eight traditions of Section 4 as analytical prose through the chapter’s analytical lens of six recurring questions. Each tradition is allowed to conclude on its own terms; explicit integration with the earlier chapters is reserved for the closing sections, in keeping with the Literature Landscape’s separation of evidence from theory. Unlike B02, which examined the engineering of knowledge systems, and B03, which examined knowledge as enduring collective capability, this chapter investigates the mature traditions that govern change itself. Its purpose is not to develop a governance methodology, but to establish what existing scholarship already contributes toward understanding how knowledge change is authorised, validated, preserved and controlled.</p>
<h3 id="executive-summary">Executive Summary</h3>
<p>Knowledge does not remain static. Scientific theories are revised, ontologies evolve, records are appraised, software is versioned, beliefs are updated, and machine-learning models are retrained. Across many disciplines, mature mechanisms already exist for governing these changes. Yet they have largely developed independently, each addressing only a particular layer of knowledge change, and rarely in conversation with one another.</p>
<p>This chapter investigates those governance traditions through a common analytical lens. Rather than asking how knowledge is represented (B02) or how it becomes collective capability (B03), it asks a different question: how is knowledge change governed? It examines eight traditions — belief revision, truth maintenance, ontology evolution, provenance, configuration and version control, records governance, knowledge quality, and AI model governance — and asks, of each, what governance capability it contributes, where it overlaps with the others, and what it leaves unresolved.</p>
<p>The central observation the chapter advances — offered as the review’s interpretation rather than as a claim of the traditions themselves — is that the primitives of governed change are, individually, mature. Belief revision supplies the logic of consistent change; truth maintenance the machinery of dependency and retraction; provenance the record of derivation; configuration management the discipline of controlled, reversible release; records governance the theory of appraisal and authenticity over time; knowledge quality the criteria of acceptance; and AI model governance the first attempts to carry all of this into the parametric layer. What is not established is whether these layer-specific mechanisms compose into a coherent architecture for governing change across layers and communities at once. Consistent with the Literature Landscape methodology, that question — whether governed knowledge change is an unsolved problem or an unintegrated one — is reserved for C01.</p>
<p>As with the preceding chapters, this document defines evidence rather than theory, and establishes part of the burden of proof that any later methodology would have to satisfy.</p>
<h3 id="orientation-for-the-standalone-reader">Orientation for the Standalone Reader</h3>
<p>This chapter builds upon the previous two evidence reviews but is intended to be read independently.</p>
<p>B02 examined how Knowledge Engineering progressively expanded the object being engineered — from expert reasoning to formal knowledge to knowledge-intensive systems to shared meaning to connected ecosystems and, most recently, to hybrid systems that combine symbolic representations (documents, rules, ontologies, graphs) with the parametric representations of machine-learning models. It concluded that the constituent technical capabilities for governed knowledge systems are individually mature but not yet integrated.</p>
<p>B03 examined complementary social and institutional traditions, arguing that they explain how knowledge becomes enduring collective capability through learning, stewardship, participation, justification and governance.</p>
<p>This chapter investigates a different dimension. Rather than asking how knowledge is engineered, or how it becomes collective capability, it asks how change itself is governed: how a proposed alteration to what a system or institution holds is authorised, validated, traced, released and preserved, so that trust survives the change. Where terms such as “justified state,” “layer” or “governed evolution” appear below, they carry the sense given in B02; a reader who has not read the earlier chapters loses none of this chapter’s argument.</p>
<h3 id="introduction">1. Introduction</h3>
<p>Knowledge continually changes. New evidence emerges, errors are corrected, classifications evolve, records accumulate, models are retrained, and communities revise what they take to be settled. The ability to govern these transitions — to change what is held while keeping it trustworthy — is fundamental to every mature knowledge discipline.</p>
<p>Yet the governance of change has never emerged as a single unified field. Instead, different scholarly traditions developed specialised mechanisms addressing particular aspects of it. Some emphasise logical consistency, some the traceability of origin, others authenticity, versioning, quality or authority. Each grew up around a particular kind of knowledge object — a belief set, an ontology, a database, a workflow, a software system, an institutional record, a dataset, a trained model — and around a particular professional community, with its own vocabulary and standards.</p>
<p>The purpose of this chapter is to investigate these governance traditions comparatively rather than individually: to place them side by side, ask the same questions of each, and see what governance capabilities they hold in common, where they diverge, and what — taken together — they do not yet address.</p>
<h3 id="central-research-question">2. Central Research Question</h3>
<p>The chapter is organised around one guiding question.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>How is knowledge change governed?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Supporting questions include: What constitutes legitimate knowledge change? How are revisions authorised? How is provenance maintained? How is authenticity preserved? How are previous knowledge states retained? How is trust maintained through change? And which governance mechanisms have become mature across disciplines?</p>
<h3 id="analytical-lens">3. Analytical Lens</h3>
<p>Each tradition is examined through six recurring analytical questions — a flexible lens rather than a rigid scoring scheme. As is normal in scholarly writing, some traditions invite fuller treatment of one question than another; the lens structures comparison without forcing equal coverage of every dimension.</p>
