<h3 id="corpus-of-investigation">4. Corpus of Investigation</h3>
<p>Unlike B02, whose corpus consisted of engineering traditions, and B03, whose corpus consisted of adjacent knowledge traditions, the corpus here consists of eight traditions concerned primarily with governing knowledge change. Each is now examined in full through the analytical lens of Section 3.</p>
<h4 id="belief-revision">4.1 Belief Revision</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How should rational beliefs change when new evidence appears?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> Belief revision is the formal study of how a rational body of belief should change under new information. Its canonical form is the AGM theory, named for Alchourrón, Gärdenfors and Makinson, whose 1985 paper set out the logic of theory change through a small set of rationality postulates (Alchourrón, Gärdenfors &amp; Makinson 1985). Gärdenfors developed the framework at book length in <em>Knowledge in Flux</em> (Gärdenfors 1988), and a large subsequent literature has refined and generalised it.</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What changes is a logically structured body of belief — a belief set or theory — as new information arrives, sometimes in conflict with what is already held.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> The difficulty is to incorporate new information while preserving consistency and giving up as little as possible. When a new belief contradicts existing ones, something must be retracted; the question is what, and on what principled basis, so that the result is both consistent and minimally disruptive.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> AGM distinguishes three operations — expansion (adding a belief), contraction (removing one) and revision (adding one while restoring consistency) — and constrains them with rationality postulates, chief among them the principle of <em>minimal change</em>: a rational agent surrenders as little of its prior commitment as consistency allows. This gives the field its enduring contribution: a precise, normative account of what it means for one justified state of belief to give way rationally to another.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> Classical AGM idealises: it assumes a well-defined logical representation, a single agent, and belief measured without regard to source, cost or authority. It says nothing about who is entitled to authorise a change, how the change is recorded for later inspection, or how belief change is managed across time and custody. It is a theory of the logic of change, not of its governance as a social or institutional process.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> Belief revision provides the logical core that several other traditions presuppose. Truth maintenance (4.2) is, in part, its computational realisation; provenance (4.4) records the derivations that revision leaves implicit; and knowledge quality (4.7) supplies the acceptance criteria that revision assumes are already settled.</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> Belief revision offers the most rigorous available grammar for a justified transition — the operations by which one consistent state becomes another with minimal loss. Any system that claims to govern change to a body of knowledge is, at some level, performing revision, and would do well to inherit AGM’s discipline of minimal, principled change. The demanding extension is that real knowledge systems are only partly logical: they hold representations that are graph-structured, statistical or parametric, for which the clean postulates of AGM have no direct analogue, and in which “minimal change” is far harder to define.</p>
<h4 id="truth-maintenance-systems">4.2 Truth Maintenance Systems</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How can a knowledge system remain logically consistent after revision?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> Truth maintenance systems (TMS), also called reason maintenance systems, are the computational counterpart to belief revision, developed within artificial intelligence. Doyle’s original TMS recorded the justifications linking beliefs, so that when one was withdrawn the consequences could be recomputed (Doyle 1979). De Kleer’s assumption-based TMS (ATMS) generalised this by tracking the sets of assumptions under which beliefs hold, allowing a system to reason about several possible states at once (de Kleer 1986).</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What changes is the set of currently held beliefs in a working reasoning system, together with the dependency structure that records why each is held.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> When a belief is retracted or added, its consequences must be updated consistently and efficiently. Recomputing everything from scratch is infeasible; the system needs to know exactly what depended on what, so that retraction propagates correctly and no longer-supported belief silently persists.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> The enduring contribution is <em>dependency-directed maintenance</em>: the explicit recording of justifications (Doyle) or assumption sets (de Kleer) so that consistency can be restored incrementally after change, and so that the support for any belief can be inspected. The ATMS adds the ability to hold multiple contexts simultaneously, distinguishing what follows under different assumptions without committing to one.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> TMS techniques are computationally expensive at scale and assume a symbolic, rule-like representation. They maintain consistency but do not decide whether a change is warranted, who may make it, or how it should be preserved; they are mechanisms of propagation, not of authority or stewardship.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> TMS operationalises belief revision (4.1) and anticipates provenance (4.4): the justification network is, in effect, a fine-grained provenance record of a reasoning process. It shares with configuration management (4.5) the concern for tracking dependencies through change.</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> Truth maintenance contributes the idea that every held belief should carry its support with it, so that change can propagate correctly and any state can be explained by the justifications behind it. This is a powerful primitive for governed change — a system that records why it holds what it holds can revise itself coherently. The open challenge is to extend dependency tracking beyond symbolic rules to the mixed symbolic–parametric representations of contemporary systems, where the “justification” for a piece of knowledge may lie in training data rather than in an inspectable inference.</p>
<h4 id="ontology-evolution">4.3 Ontology Evolution</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How should conceptual structures evolve over time?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> Ontology evolution is the study of how shared conceptual models — the classes, relations and axioms through which a domain is represented — should change while remaining usable. Noy and Klein argued that ontology evolution is not the same as database schema evolution, because ontologies carry richer semantics and are reused by distributed, independent parties (Noy &amp; Klein 2004). Flouris and colleagues surveyed and classified the varieties of ontology change (Flouris et al. 2008), and a related strand, including work associated with Stuckenschmidt and with Klein and Fensel, addressed ontology versioning and compatibility on the Semantic Web (Klein &amp; Fensel 2001). The database tradition on schema versioning and temporal data (Roddick 1995; Snodgrass 1999) is the close technical cousin of this work at the data layer.</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What changes is the conceptual model itself — the schema of meaning — as understanding of a domain develops, corrects itself or expands.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> Changing an ontology can invalidate the data described by it, the mappings that connect it to other ontologies, and the dependent systems that consume it. Because ontologies are reused by parties the author does not control, a change can ripple unpredictably. The tradition must therefore distinguish evolution (changing while keeping the ontology and its instances consistent) from versioning (tracking the identity and compatibility of successive versions), and manage the propagation of change.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> The tradition contributes a vocabulary and taxonomy of ontology change, the crucial recognition that meaning-level change differs from structural change, and a set of versioning and compatibility strategies — change logs, impact analysis, and compatibility relations between versions — for keeping a conceptual model usable as it evolves.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> Tooling remains immature; preserving semantics across change is hard; and distributed reuse means the full effect of a change is often unknowable in advance. The tradition addresses the mechanics of change well but says little about the authority to make it, or about appraisal — which changes are worth making and which should be resisted.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> Ontology evolution extends belief revision (4.1) from individual beliefs to the schema that gives them meaning, and shares versioning concerns with configuration management (4.5) and the schema-evolution strand of databases. It depends on provenance (4.4) to record why a conceptual change was made.</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> Ontology evolution is change governance at the level of shared meaning — the layer that sits between raw data and human concepts. Its evolution/versioning distinction, and its insistence that semantic change is not reducible to structural change, are directly inheritable. The open challenge, and one that recurs throughout this chapter, is coordinating change at the meaning layer with change at the data layer beneath it and the model layer beside it, so that a revision in one does not silently invalidate the others.</p>
<h4 id="provenance">4.4 Provenance</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How should knowledge changes remain traceable?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> Provenance is the record of where knowledge came from and how it was produced. The database tradition characterised data provenance precisely — Buneman, Khanna and Tan distinguished “why” and “where” provenance, identifying which source data explained a result and where it originated (Buneman, Khanna &amp; Tan 2001). The e-science community developed workflow provenance, capturing the computational steps by which a result was derived (Davidson &amp; Freire 2008). These strands converged in an interoperability standard, the W3C PROV data model, which represents provenance in terms of entities, activities and agents so that it can be exchanged across systems (Moreau &amp; Missier 2013).</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What is recorded is the origin and derivation of a piece of knowledge — the trail connecting a current state to the sources, processes and actors that produced it.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> Without provenance, the trustworthiness of a result cannot be assessed, reproduced or audited, and a change cannot be explained after the fact. Capturing derivation across heterogeneous queries, workflows and systems — at a useful granularity and in a form others can interpret — is genuinely hard.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> The tradition contributes precise characterisations of provenance (why-, where- and how-provenance), techniques for capturing the derivation of computational results, and, in PROV, a shared vocabulary that lets provenance travel between systems. Its deeper contribution is the principle that a knowledge state should be accompanied by an inspectable account of how it came to be.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> Provenance capture is costly and frequently incomplete; there are hard trade-offs of granularity and storage; and the provenance of machine-learning outputs — whose derivation is diffuse across training data and parameters — remains only partially tractable. Standards adoption is uneven.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> Provenance is the connective tissue of change governance: it records the <em>why</em> behind changes that belief revision performs, that configuration management releases, and that records governance preserves. It is the computational descendant of archival provenance and respect des fonds (4.6), and closely related to the justification networks of truth maintenance (4.2).</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> Provenance is arguably the single most important primitive for governed change: it is what makes a transition auditable and a state’s justification inspectable after the fact. A system that governs change must carry provenance across all its layers — including the hard case of parametric knowledge, where derivation cannot be read off an inference but must be reconstructed from data and training history. Inheriting provenance is not optional for a governed knowledge system; extending it to the model layer is one of its central unsolved problems.</p>
<h4 id="configuration-and-version-control">4.5 Configuration and Version Control</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How should controlled releases be governed?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> Software configuration management (SCM) is the engineering discipline of controlling change to complex, evolving artefacts. Conradi and Westfechtel’s survey of version models gave the field its conceptual map — versions, variants and configurations, and the operations that relate them (Conradi &amp; Westfechtel 1998). In practice the tradition is realised in version-control systems, branching and merging workflows, baselines, and the release-engineering practices that govern what is published and when.</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What changes is a complex artefact composed of many interdependent parts, altered concurrently by many contributors over time.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> The difficulty is to coordinate concurrent change without losing consistency or history: to let many people modify an artefact in parallel, reconcile their changes, control what is released, and be able to return to any previous state.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> SCM contributes the most mature account anywhere of controlled, attributable, reversible change: version models that capture how states relate; the disciplines of check-in and check-out, branching and merging, baselines and controlled releases; and the principle that every change is recorded, attributable to an author, and — crucially — reversible. Release engineering adds the governance of when and what to publish, separating work in progress from what is declared trustworthy.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> SCM was designed for files and code, where change is textual and merges are largely mechanical. Merging <em>semantic</em> change — two incompatible revisions of meaning rather than of text — is far harder, and rollback of semantic change can leave dependent knowledge inconsistent. The release discipline is also process-heavy, presupposing human review.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> Configuration management provides the process backbone the other traditions lack: the release and rollback machinery that turns a proposed change into a governed one. It shares versioning concerns with ontology and schema evolution (4.3), and its check-in review is a form of the authority that records governance (4.6) exercises through appraisal.</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> SCM contributes the discipline of controlled release — proposal, review, baseline, publication and rollback — which a governed knowledge system can borrow largely intact as the process spine of governed change. What SCM does not solve, and such a system must, is <em>semantic merge and rollback</em>: reconciling and, if necessary, undoing changes to meaning-bearing knowledge whose parts are interdependent in ways that text is not.</p>
<h4 id="records-governance">4.6 Records Governance</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How should authenticity survive continual change?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> Records governance — the archival and records-management tradition — is the institutional theory of keeping records trustworthy over time. Its classical poles are Jenkinson’s custodial account, with its emphasis on the impartiality and authenticity of records (Jenkinson 1922), and Schellenberg’s theory of appraisal, which asks what records have enduring value and should be retained (Schellenberg 1956). The modern tradition adds Duranti’s diplomatics-based analysis of reliability and authenticity (Duranti 1995), the records continuum’s treatment of change as constitutive rather than incidental (Upward 1996), Cook’s account of shifting archival paradigms (Cook 2013), and digital-preservation reference models such as OAIS for maintaining authenticity across technological change (CCSDS 2012). Information governance is the contemporary organisational umbrella for these concerns.</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What is governed is the institution’s records over time — what is captured, appraised, retained, preserved authentically, and eventually disposed of.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> Not everything can or should be kept. The tradition must decide what to retain (appraisal), preserve the authenticity and reliability of what is kept as it passes through custodians and formats, and maintain an unbroken chain of evidence across decades or centuries — a far longer horizon than any computational tradition contemplates.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> Records governance contributes what the technical traditions omit: appraisal (a principled account of deciding what has enduring value), the paired concepts of reliability and authenticity (Duranti), provenance and respect des fonds as organising principles, and reference models for sustaining authenticity through format and custody change. Its deeper contribution is to treat trust-over-time as the central problem rather than an afterthought.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> Appraisal is inescapably value-laden and labour-intensive; the tradition is historically document-centric; and adapting its methods to dynamic, computational and machine-generated records is an ongoing project rather than a settled achievement.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> Records governance is the institutional counterpart to the technical traditions: where provenance (4.4) records derivation and configuration management (4.5) controls release, appraisal decides what is worth keeping and authenticity certifies that what is kept can be trusted. It asks the question — what should we retain? — that belief revision and truth maintenance never pose.</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> Records governance contributes appraisal and authenticity over long horizons — precisely the problem of keeping knowledge trustworthy as it changes, which the computational traditions treat only in passing. A governed knowledge system should inherit appraisal (deciding what to promote, retain and retire) and the reliability/authenticity apparatus, and extend them from documents to hybrid symbolic–parametric knowledge, where establishing that a model or dataset is what it purports to be, and has not been tampered with, is markedly harder than for a record.</p>
<h4 id="knowledge-quality">4.7 Knowledge Quality</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> When should knowledge be accepted?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> The knowledge-quality tradition — spanning information, data and knowledge quality — asks by what criteria a body of knowledge is judged fit for use. Wang and Strong’s foundational study derived quality dimensions from the perspective of the data consumer, grouping them into intrinsic, contextual, representational and accessibility categories (Wang &amp; Strong 1996). Redman connected data quality to organisational and economic consequences (Redman 1996), and Batini and colleagues later surveyed the methodologies developed for assessing and improving it (Batini et al. 2009).</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What is assessed is the fitness-for-use of data and information — the criteria by which a candidate knowledge state is judged acceptable or not.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> When should a piece of knowledge be accepted, and when rejected? Quality is multi-dimensional and relative to use: the same data may be adequate for one purpose and inadequate for another. The tradition must therefore make quality assessable rather than merely asserted.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> The tradition contributes multi-dimensional models of quality — accuracy, completeness, timeliness, consistency, relevance and more, organised into coherent categories — together with assessment and improvement methodologies. Its conceptual advance is the reframing of quality as <em>fitness-for-use</em> rather than intrinsic accuracy, which makes acceptance a judgement relative to purpose and consumer rather than an absolute.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> Because quality is context-dependent, it resists fully objective measurement; assessment is resource-intensive; and the tradition says more about whether knowledge is fit than about who is authorised to declare it so.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> Knowledge quality supplies the acceptance criteria that gate change — the validation step that belief revision (4.1), provenance (4.4) and controlled release (4.5) all presuppose but do not themselves define. It complements records appraisal (4.6): quality asks whether knowledge is fit for present use, appraisal whether it has enduring value.</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> Knowledge quality contributes the validation dimension of governed change — the criteria by which a proposed new state is judged fit to become the accepted one. A controlled transition from one justified state to another needs an explicit account of acceptance, and this tradition supplies its most developed vocabulary. What it leaves open, and any such system must address, is how fitness-for-use is assessed for knowledge whose consumers and purposes are many and changing, and for parametric knowledge whose quality cannot be inspected directly.</p>
<h4 id="ai-model-governance">4.8 AI Model Governance</h4>
<p><strong>Central question:</strong> How should machine-learning systems evolve responsibly?</p>
<p><strong>Development and key figures.</strong> AI model governance is the youngest of the traditions surveyed here, and the one most directly concerned with the parametric layer that B02 identified as newly central. Standardised documentation artefacts have been its most visible contribution: model cards, which report a model’s intended use, performance across conditions and limitations (Mitchell et al. 2019), and datasheets for datasets, which document a dataset’s motivation, composition and collection (Gebru et al. 2021). Alongside these sit the recognition of machine-learning-specific maintenance burdens and “technical debt” (Sculley et al. 2015), the extension of established model risk management practice from finance (as codified in supervisory guidance such as SR 11-7) to machine learning, and risk-management frameworks such as the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (NIST 2023).</p>
<p><strong>Primary object.</strong> What is governed is the knowledge embedded in machine-learning models and their datasets — how it is documented, evaluated, released and controlled across a lifecycle of training, deployment and retraining.</p>
<p><strong>Primary problem.</strong> Parametric knowledge is opaque, data-dependent and prone to drift; retraining can change a model’s behaviour in ways that are hard to predict or explain; and accountability for those changes is difficult to assign. Governing change responsibly, when the knowledge in question cannot be read directly, is the core challenge.</p>
<p><strong>Core contribution.</strong> The tradition’s contribution so far is to make model and dataset knowledge legible through standardised documentation (model cards, datasheets), to import lifecycle and risk-management discipline from older fields, and to name the specific maintenance and governance problems of machine-learning systems. It is, in effect, an attempt to bring provenance, appraisal, quality assessment and controlled release to bear on the parametric layer.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining limitations.</strong> The tradition is nascent. Documentation is largely voluntary and unevenly practised; parametric knowledge resists provenance and versioning in the ways the older traditions assume; evaluation of drift, bias and harm is immature; and the governing standards are still forming rather than settled.</p>
<p><strong>Relationship to other traditions.</strong> AI model governance is best understood as importing the other traditions into a new setting: model cards and datasheets are a form of provenance and documentation (4.4); lifecycle management echoes configuration management (4.5); model risk management echoes appraisal and quality (4.6, 4.7); and the underlying problem — governing change to knowledge that is held implicitly — is a parametric analogue of belief revision (4.1).</p>
<p><strong>Relevance to governed knowledge evolution.</strong> AI model governance is where change governance meets the parametric layer, and therefore where the cross-layer ambition of governed knowledge evolution is most severely tested. Its documentation artefacts gesture toward provenance and appraisal for models, but governing change to parametric knowledge with the rigour the older traditions bring to symbolic knowledge remains substantially unsolved. A governed knowledge system should inherit the tradition’s instinct — that model and dataset knowledge must be documented, evaluated and released under governance — while recognising that the mechanisms for doing so are the least mature of any surveyed here.</p>
<h3 id="comparative-analysis">5. Comparative Analysis</h3>
<p>Compared through the lens of Section 3, the eight traditions resolve into a small, recurring set of <strong>Governance Primitives</strong> — the elementary functions that any act of governed change performs. Six recur across the traditions clearly enough to be named as a set:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>The Six Governance Primitives</strong> - <strong>Evidence / Justification</strong> — the grounds on which a change is made - <strong>Authority</strong> — who is entitled to make it - <strong>Provenance / Traceability</strong> — the record of what changed and why - <strong>Validation / Quality</strong> — the criteria by which the new state is judged acceptable - <strong>Versioning / Release</strong> — how the new state is published and distinguished from its predecessors - <strong>Reversibility / Audit</strong> — how a change can be reviewed or undone</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These six are offered as a provisional analytical vocabulary rather than a settled taxonomy; they may prove useful beyond this chapter as a shared language for comparing governance across the Literature Landscape. The matrix below maps where each tradition contributes most strongly against them, distinguishing a principal (●) from a meaningful secondary (○) contribution. It is a map of complementarity, not a scoring exercise: a blank cell means the tradition has little to say on that primitive, not that it is deficient.</p>
