<p>AI Model Governance is marked with several secondary contributions and no principal one by design: it is an emerging tradition that imports primitives from the others into the parametric layer rather than originating any of them.</p>
<p><strong>Recurring governance principles.</strong> Across the traditions, the same primitives recur under different names. Every tradition assumes some notion of evidence or justification for a change; most record provenance or traceability; several provide validation against acceptance criteria; several manage versioning and controlled release; and several support reversibility or audit. Only two — records governance and, in a nascent way, AI model governance — foreground authority: the question of who is entitled to make a change.</p>
<p><strong>Complementary mechanisms.</strong> The traditions divide the labour of governance. Belief revision and truth maintenance govern logical consistency through change; ontology evolution governs meaning; provenance governs traceability; configuration management governs release and reversibility; records governance governs retention and authenticity over time; knowledge quality governs acceptance; and AI model governance attempts all of these for the parametric layer. Read together, they cover the space of what governing a change requires.</p>
<p><strong>Overlaps and differences in authority.</strong> The mechanisms overlap productively — provenance underlies audit, versioning enables rollback, quality gates acceptance — but they differ sharply in their treatment of authority. The computational traditions largely assume that <em>whether</em> a change is warranted is settled elsewhere; they mechanise its propagation, not its authorisation. It is the institutional tradition, records governance, that treats the decision to change — appraisal — as itself a governed act. This asymmetry is one of the chapter’s more striking findings.</p>
<p><strong>Treatment of provenance and trust over time.</strong> Provenance appears, in some form, in almost every tradition — as justification networks (truth maintenance), derivation records (data and workflow provenance), change logs (ontology and configuration management), and chains of custody (records governance). Trust over time, by contrast, is the special province of records governance and digital preservation; most computational traditions govern change in the present and treat the retention of past states as a secondary concern.</p>
<p><strong>Mature capabilities.</strong> Several capabilities are, across these traditions, genuinely mature: the logic of consistent revision (belief revision, truth maintenance); the recording of derivation (provenance); controlled, reversible release (configuration management); appraisal and authenticity over long horizons (records governance); and multi-dimensional quality assessment (knowledge quality). This maturity is the precondition for stating precisely what is not yet established.</p>
<h3 id="cross-tradition-patterns">6. Cross-Tradition Patterns</h3>
<p>The scaffold for this chapter proposed a set of candidate dimensions along which the traditions might be compared — evidence, authority, provenance, versioning, validation, release, accountability and traceability — and asked whether common governance principles emerge across otherwise independent traditions. Evaluated against the literature, they do, though not as a chronological progression.</p>
<p>Consolidating the scaffold’s candidates yields exactly the six Governance Primitives named in Section 5 — evidence/justification, authority, provenance/traceability, validation/quality, versioning/release, and reversibility/audit — offered here as a provisional pattern supported by the present review rather than a validated finding.</p>
<p>No single tradition instantiates all six. Belief revision and truth maintenance are strongest on evidence and consistency; provenance on traceability; configuration management on versioning and reversibility; records governance on authority and authenticity; knowledge quality on validation. The traditions do not form a succession in which each supersedes the last; they form a <strong>division of governance labour</strong> — an accretion of complementary primitives, each matured by a different community around a different kind of knowledge object.</p>
<p>This reading connects to the three-axis lens introduced in B03 — <strong>Scale → Capability → Time</strong>. The Governance Primitives elaborate chiefly the Capability axis (the operations by which knowledge is changed and controlled) and the Time axis (provenance, retention and authenticity, by which trust is maintained across time). The Scale axis appears more faintly, surfacing mainly where authority is at stake — the point at which governing a change becomes a social and institutional act rather than a computational one. That the computational traditions populate Capability densely while leaving Scale (authority) thin is, on this reading, the characteristic shape of the field.</p>
<h4 id="limitations-of-this-review">6.1 Limitations of This Review</h4>
<p>As with the preceding chapters, this review is a work of interpretive synthesis and its conclusions should be read with four limitations in mind.</p>
<p>First, it is a <strong>selective narrative synthesis</strong> rather than a systematic review: the eight traditions and their representative works were chosen for canonical standing and bearing on the guiding question, not assembled through an exhaustive, protocol-driven search. The absence of a tradition or source here is not evidence of its absence from the field.</p>
<p>Second, it is organised around a <strong>single interpretive lens</strong> — knowledge change and its governance. A different organising principle (for example, the economics of data, regulatory compliance, or the philosophy of scientific change) would foreground different traditions and yield a different, equally legitimate synthesis.</p>
<p>Third, it is weighted toward <strong>computational, English-language scholarship</strong>, with the institutional tradition of records governance the main counterweight. It under-represents legal, regulatory and non-Western treatments of authority over knowledge, and bodies of work in the history and sociology of science that address how accepted knowledge changes.</p>
<p>Fourth, it is <strong>not a complete treatment of any of the eight traditions</strong>, each of which sustains its own internal debates — the refinements and rivals of AGM, the varieties of provenance, the custodial–postcustodial division in archival science, the fast-moving state of AI governance — that are only gestured at here. The Position Paper and the Literature Synthesis will need to engage these literatures more fully than an evidence chapter can.</p>
<p>These limitations bound the chapter’s claims without undermining its argument: what is offered is a defensible comparative reading of the change-governance traditions and of what they contribute, not a settled or exhaustive account.</p>
<h3 id="existing-contributions">7. Existing Contributions</h3>
<p>Across these traditions, several governance capabilities can reasonably be regarded as mature bodies of scholarship rather than emerging ideas. They are set out plainly here, so that no unsupported claim of novelty can later be made on their behalf:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>logical belief revision</strong> — the rational operations of expansion, contraction and revision under minimal change (Belief Revision);</li>
<li><strong>dependency maintenance</strong> — justification networks and assumption-based contexts that keep a reasoning system consistent through change (Truth Maintenance Systems);</li>
<li><strong>ontology evolution and versioning</strong> — managed change to conceptual models, distinguished from schema change, with compatibility across versions (Ontology Evolution);</li>
<li><strong>provenance</strong> — why-, where- and how-provenance, workflow provenance, and the interoperable PROV model (Provenance);</li>
<li><strong>controlled, reversible release</strong> — version models, branching and merging, baselines and release engineering (Configuration and Version Control);</li>
<li><strong>appraisal and authenticity over time</strong> — retention decisions, reliability and authenticity, the records continuum, and digital-preservation models (Records Governance);</li>
<li><strong>quality assessment</strong> — multi-dimensional, fitness-for-use models of data and information quality and the methodologies to assess them (Knowledge Quality); and</li>
<li><strong>model and dataset governance</strong> — model cards, datasheets, lifecycle and risk-management practices for machine-learning knowledge (AI Model Governance).</li>
</ul>
<p>This inventory, together with those of B02 and B03, defines the stock of established governance capability against which any claim of novelty must be tested.</p>
<h3 id="candidate-integrative-insight">8. Candidate Integrative Insight</h3>
<p>Only after analysing the traditions can the chapter state its candidate integrative observation — and, as in the preceding chapters, it is stated with restraint, and as an interpretation advanced by the review rather than a claim drawn from the traditions themselves.</p>
<p>The traditions surveyed here do not describe themselves as a single field; each governs change to a particular kind of knowledge object, within a particular community. The interpretive move this chapter makes is to read them together and observe that, between them, they already supply mature mechanisms for every individual facet of governed change: justification and consistency, provenance, controlled and reversible release, appraisal and authenticity, and validation. On the evidence assembled here, governing a knowledge change is not, in any single layer, an unsolved problem.</p>
<p>What is not established is whether these mechanisms <strong>compose</strong>. Each was developed for a particular representational layer — logical belief, conceptual model, dataset, software artefact, institutional record, trained model — and around a particular professional community, largely in isolation from the others. They share primitives (evidence, provenance, versioning, validation) but not a common architecture, vocabulary or point of authority. The candidate integrative insight is therefore the more modest and more precise observation that governed knowledge change appears to be <strong>less an unsolved problem within any layer than an unintegrated one across layers</strong>: the primitives exist, but whether they can be composed into a coherent architecture that governs change across the human, symbolic and parametric layers at once is not something any of these traditions, alone, establishes.</p>
<p>Consistent with the Literature Landscape methodology, whether such composition is achievable — and whether achieving it would be a genuinely distinct contribution or a recombination of mature parts — is reserved for C01. This chapter’s task is the narrower evidential one: to establish what the change-governance traditions contribute, and where the seams between them lie.</p>
<h3 id="burden-of-proof">9. Burden of Proof</h3>
<p>Consistent with the Literature Landscape methodology, this chapter defines evidence, not theory. It establishes the portion of the evidential burden — arising from the traditions surveyed here — that any methodology claiming to govern continuously evolving knowledge would have to discharge, structured around the three standard questions.</p>
<p><strong>Established (what is mature).</strong> Each facet of governed change is mature within its own layer: the logic of consistent revision (belief revision, truth maintenance); the recording of derivation (provenance); controlled, reversible release (configuration management); appraisal and authenticity over long horizons (records governance); and multi-dimensional quality assessment (knowledge quality).</p>
<p><strong>Unresolved (what remains open).</strong> These mechanisms have not been shown to compose across layers. On the evidence of this chapter, a methodology governing continuously evolving knowledge would need to show that it can:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>authorise change</strong> — specify who or what is entitled to alter the accepted state, a question only records governance foregrounds (Records Governance);</li>
<li><strong>validate change</strong> — judge a proposed state fit to be accepted, by explicit criteria (Knowledge Quality);</li>
<li><strong>preserve provenance</strong> — carry an inspectable record of what changed and why (Provenance; Truth Maintenance Systems);</li>
<li><strong>maintain trust over time</strong> — preserve authenticity and reliability across custody and format change (Records Governance);</li>
<li><strong>record history</strong> — retain and reconstruct previous states (Configuration and Version Control; the temporal-data strand of Ontology Evolution);</li>
<li><strong>coordinate multiple governance layers</strong> — reconcile change across logical, conceptual, data, artefact, institutional and parametric representations, which no surveyed tradition does; and</li>
<li><strong>accommodate human and machine knowledge simultaneously</strong> — govern symbolic and parametric knowledge under one regime, which AI model governance only begins to attempt (AI Model Governance).</li>
</ul>
<p>None of the surveyed traditions claims responsibility for all seven simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>Implication (what the synthesis must explain).</strong> The synthesis must determine whether the six Governance Primitives can be composed into one architecture governing change across the human, symbolic and parametric layers at once — and whether doing so is a genuinely distinct contribution or a recombination of mature parts. This chapter asserts neither; the verdict is left to the Position Paper and to C01.</p>
<h3 id="relationship-to-previous-and-subsequent-chapters">10. Relationship to Previous and Subsequent Chapters</h3>
<p>B02 examined the engineering of knowledge systems; B03 examined how knowledge becomes enduring collective capability; B04 examines how change to that knowledge is governed. Together the three chapters assemble three complementary bodies of evidence — engineering, collective capability, and the governance of change — none sufficient alone. B04 stands in a particularly close relation to B02, whose survey of knowledge-revision traditions it deepens and extends: where B02 noted these mechanisms in passing as evidence that change can be engineered layer by layer, this chapter examines them in their own right and finds the same pattern of layer-confined maturity. The remaining evidence chapters extend the survey further, and it is the Literature Synthesis, rather than any single chapter, that weighs the combined evidence and identifies the overall gap, if any.</p>
<h3 id="guiding-principle">11. Guiding Principle</h3>
<p>The organising principle of this chapter is intentionally simple.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Knowledge systems remain trustworthy only because their changes are governed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Knowledge systems do not merely accumulate information; they continuously change, and it is the governance of that change — its authorisation, validation, provenance, release and audit — that allows trust to survive it. The mature traditions surveyed here show that governing knowledge change has long been recognised as fundamental. But they show something more particular too: that the labour has been divided. Each tradition matured one part of governance within one layer of knowledge, so that what exists today is a division of governance labour rather than a single architecture — and the governance of change across layers is the question these traditions leave for others to answer.</p>
<p><em>The emergence of foundation models raises the question of whether these governance mechanisms remain adequate. B05 investigates this directly.</em></p>
